1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
Coach: A combined proposal should make its connections easy to follow.

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:08,571
Coach: Show the main sequence, explain how it serves the aims,

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:11,143
Coach: and keep the important checks nearby.

4
00:00:11,143 --> 00:00:14,143
Coach: You are giving people something to inspect,

5
00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:20,571
Coach: not presenting a polished diagram as proof that the discussion has already reached a decision.

6
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:25,635
Coach: This is a later authored check of the book-club candidate.

7
00:00:25,635 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: The earlier partial support remains the starting point.

8
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:33,350
Coach: The next lines supply new statements for this example only;

9
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:38,921
Coach: a person must actually say what they support before their status card changes.

10
00:00:39,271 --> 00:00:44,842
You: We could begin with a brief example, use the same text in pairs,

11
00:00:44,842 --> 00:00:49,985
You: share one idea, and keep at least five minutes for club questions.

12
00:00:49,985 --> 00:00:55,985
You: Can we check the timing and who would give the example before we decide?

13
00:00:56,335 --> 00:01:00,621
Coach: The proposed example and pair discussion use the same text,

14
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:06,192
Coach: so they are connected stages rather than separate activities competing for a place.

15
00:01:06,192 --> 00:01:09,192
Coach: Sharing would draw on the pair task.

16
00:01:09,192 --> 00:01:13,906
Coach: The candidate still needs timing and someone to give the example;

17
00:01:13,906 --> 00:01:18,192
Coach: mentioning those checks does not mean they have been completed.

18
00:01:26,542 --> 00:01:32,542
Coach: A focused check asks about an element or a condition that is still open.

19
00:01:32,542 --> 00:01:37,685
Coach: Do you support this sequence, or is there something you would change?

20
00:01:37,685 --> 00:01:39,399
Coach: That is more useful

21
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:44,542
Coach: than asking everyone to approve a summary that already says everyone agrees.

22
00:01:44,542 --> 00:01:47,971
Coach: Leave space for a limited or negative answer.

23
00:01:48,321 --> 00:01:52,178
Maya: I support that sequence if the demonstration stays brief

24
00:01:52,178 --> 00:01:54,321
Maya: and the pair task remains.

25
00:01:54,671 --> 00:01:58,100
Coach: Maya has now supported the sequence, with conditions.

26
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:03,671
Coach: Record the whole statement: the example stays brief and the pair task remains.

27
00:02:03,671 --> 00:02:06,671
Coach: Do not turn it into unconditional support,

28
00:02:06,671 --> 00:02:13,100
Coach: and do not treat her response as an answer to who would lead the example.

29
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:17,736
Noah: I support starting with an example and keeping question time.

30
00:02:17,736 --> 00:02:24,164
Noah: I am not ready to agree on the full sequence until we check the timings.

31
00:02:24,514 --> 00:02:28,371
Coach: Noah's reply is not a rejection of every part.

32
00:02:28,371 --> 00:02:30,943
Coach: It preserves support for two elements

33
00:02:30,943 --> 00:02:35,657
Coach: and gives a reason for withholding support for the complete arrangement.

34
00:02:35,657 --> 00:02:39,943
Coach: Your summary should be able to hold that mixed position

35
00:02:39,943 --> 00:02:43,800
Coach: without colouring the whole row simply yes or no.

36
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:46,721
Coach: The current record has moved forward,

37
00:02:46,721 --> 00:02:50,579
Coach: but it is still not a final group decision.

38
00:02:50,579 --> 00:02:57,436
Coach: A useful next step is to draft stage lengths, include transitions and protect the question period,

39
00:02:57,436 --> 00:03:00,864
Coach: then ask whether the result addresses the reservation.

40
00:03:00,864 --> 00:03:05,150
Coach: Someone also needs to check who could give the example.

41
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:12,357
Coach: If you offer to make that draft, present it as an offer rather than a command.

42
00:03:12,357 --> 00:03:17,500
Coach: Nothing in this case appoints a leader or establishes a voting rule.

43
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:21,357
Coach: The group can decide how to do the check;

44
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:25,643
Coach: the summariser should not quietly claim authority over that decision.

45
00:03:25,993 --> 00:03:30,279
You: Would it help if we sketched the stage lengths next,

46
00:03:30,279 --> 00:03:34,136
You: including transitions, and then checked whether the sequence fits?

47
00:03:34,486 --> 00:03:37,914
Coach: An offer without a reply remains an offer.

48
00:03:37,914 --> 00:03:41,771
Coach: Keep that distinction when you hand over the summary.

49
00:03:41,771 --> 00:03:45,629
Coach: Otherwise, a proposed action can quietly become someone's obligation,

50
00:03:45,629 --> 00:03:49,057
Coach: just as partial support can quietly become consensus.

51
00:04:01,407 --> 00:04:06,550
Coach: A helpful handover lets someone see the candidate, whose support is conditional,

52
00:04:06,550 --> 00:04:10,407
Coach: what remains unresolved and what check is being proposed.

53
00:04:10,407 --> 00:04:16,407
Coach: Keep it concise enough to use, but not so short that the conditions disappear.

54
00:04:16,407 --> 00:04:20,693
Coach: The next speaker should be able to correct one item

55
00:04:20,693 --> 00:04:22,836
Coach: without rebuilding the whole account.

56
00:04:23,186 --> 00:04:27,900
Coach: The point of checking is not to collect a polite yes.

57
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:34,329
Coach: It is to make the group's actual position clear enough for a useful next decision.

58
00:04:34,329 --> 00:04:36,043
Coach: In the next section,

59
00:04:36,043 --> 00:04:43,329
Coach: practise giving that kind of summary while keeping your own private rehearsal separate from the authored example.
